# OpenWith and Shortcut design


A main can ask the Fearless manager to make the desktop open it: on a double click on any file with a given extension (`OpenWith`), or on a small file the manager creates for it (`Shortcut`).

```
Foo: Main, OpenWith[IconsFoo, "foo"]{ s -> ... }
Bar: Main, Shortcut[IconsBar, "bar"]{ s -> ... }
Baz: Main, OpenWith[IconsBaz]{ s -> ... }
Qux: Main, Shortcut[IconsQux]{ s -> ... }
```

`IconsFoo` and the others are types the compiler generates for image files recognised as autoloaded assets (here `_pkg/icons/foo.png` becomes `IconsFoo`). They can come from any package the main can see, including `base` once `base` offers autoloaded icons.

Informally: `Foo` wants every `.foo` file to show the `IconsFoo` image and to open `Foo` on a double click. `Bar` wants a file `bar.bar` in the top level of its project, showing the `IconsBar` image, that starts `Bar` on a double click; that file carries no information, it is only a handle to start the main, and it keeps working when moved anywhere else.

Mechanically `OpenWith` and `Shortcut` are the same: both claim an extension for a main. `Shortcut` additionally creates the shortcut file.

The manager registers every claimed extension to itself. The desktop starts the manager on the double clicked file; the manager looks up which mains claim that extension and runs the right one.

A missing extension is chosen by the manager (see **Autoselected extensions**). In this case, extensions are meant to be hidden or ignored by users, who recognise their files by the icon.

Out of scope: macOS (no associations exist there today), delivering the opened file to the program (see **Delivering the file**), and feeding more files to a running main.

## Base

```
OpenWith[I,E]: {}
OpenWith[I]: {}
Shortcut[I,E]: {}
Shortcut[I]: {}
```

`Shortcut` does not extend `OpenWith`.

A main can implement several of them at once, for example `OpenWith[Icon1,"a"]` and `OpenWith[Icon2,"b"]` to open two extensions; the icons can differ or be the same. Several `Shortcut` produce several shortcut files for the same main. Implementing the same type twice (`Shortcut[I]` inherited along two paths) counts once.

## Well formedness (Frontend)

These checks live next to the `Sealed`, `WidenTo` and `BaseId` checks, in `inject.Methods.checkMagicSupertypes`, with their errors in `WellFormednessErrors`. They are well formedness rules on the supertype closure `allCs`, not typing rules, so the paper type system is unaffected.

1. Every declaration whose supertype closure contains `base.OpenWith[_]`, `base.OpenWith[_,_]`, `base.Shortcut[_]` or `base.Shortcut[_,_]` also has `base.Main` in its supertype closure. This goes in the opposite direction to `Sealed`: the implementer, not the implemented type, must carry something. Since the closure is transitive, the declaration blamed is the one that first adds `OpenWith`/`Shortcut` without `Main`, even when it is meant to be mixed into a main later. The four base declarations themselves are exempt.
   Wording, in the style of `extendedSealed`:
   `Type "Assoc" implements "base.OpenWith[_,_]".`
   `Only a main can open files: "Assoc" must also implement "base.Main", directly or through one of its supertypes.`
2. The arguments are concrete: the first is a type name with no generic arguments, the second a `base` string literal type. No type variables, so every check below can be done on the declaration itself.
3. The extension literal: the text between the delimiters, with `"txt"` and `` `txt` `` meaning the same extension `txt`. The two are different type names, so the rule compares the text, and a main claiming both `OpenWith[I,"txt"]` and `` OpenWith[I,`txt`] `` is claiming `txt` twice (rule 5).
   The text follows the rule for a single extension segment of a file in a project (`BuildWithZip.checkExtSeg`): 1 to 16 characters, each `a-z` or `0-9`; no leading dot; no multi dot extensions such as `tar.gz`. That rule is in Coordinator, which Frontend does not depend on: it moves into Commons (`tools.Fs`, next to `allowed`) and both use it.

Crucially, only `fearless` is reserved and can not be claimed.
`fear` can be, and a fearles IDE written in fearless may want to claim it.
Every text matching `fapp[0-9][0-9][0-9]` or `ffile[0-9][0-9][0-9]` can also be claimed.
4. The icon is a type generated by the autoloading of an image file. `base.ImageFile` is not sealed, so a hand written `ImageFile` is refused here. Whether the image is a usable PNG is checked by the manager (see **Checking the icon**).
5. One main claims each extension at most once, across all its `OpenWith` and `Shortcut`: `Foo: Main, OpenWith[I1,"a"], Shortcut[I2,"a"]` is refused, since one extension has one icon.

Only mains the manager can run take part: the concrete mains of the highest rank of the project (`Helper.isMain`, `Helper.compile`). `OpenWith`/`Shortcut` on a main of a lower rank is legal and has no effect.

## What the compiler records (Coordinator, portable and manager alike)

The portable Fearless does nothing with `OpenWith`/`Shortcut`; the compiler is shared, so it records them in either case, and only the manager reads them.

What today is `<pkg>.mains`
need to become a file called `.fearless_out/mains.info` using Info format to save more data.
 {
"hello.Bar":["_hello/bar.fear",["_hello/icons/shortcut.png","bar"],["_hello/icons/icon3.png","fapp342"],
"hello.Beer":["_hello/beer.fear",[],[]],
}
Where each Main is associated to the list of all extensions; first the list of Shortcuts then the list of OpenWith. Those lists can be empty no problem.
The manager running program should then construct the opposite map (extension->list of mains claiming it) but not save it on disk; keep it in memory and reconstruct it from the managed .fearless_outs on manager restart.)

The address of the icon in the example may be too simplistic. For each icon type the compiler also records where the image is: the `.diskPath`, `.zipSteps` and `.zipEntry` of the asset, relative to the root of the project owning the icon's package (the stdLib folder for `base`). An icon from another package of the project is resolved from that package's autoloaded assets, not from the current package's `CapabilityEnvironment`, which only knows its own.

## What the manager does after a compile

The compile runs in the child JVM (`ChildMain`); everything below runs in the manager process after the child succeeded, because it needs the claims of every other project.

1. Read the claims of the project from its `<pkg>.mains`.
2. **Checking the icon.** Each icon must be a PNG file (autoloading also accepts jpg, gif and bmp; they are refused here), square, with a side from 64 to 1024 pixels. A failure is reported as the compile failing, worded like a Frontend error and naming the main, the icon type and the image file:
   `The icon "hello.IconsBar" in "base.Shortcut[hello.IconsBar,\"bar\"]" of main "hello.Bar" is 300x200 pixels (from "_hello/icons/bar.png"): an icon must be a square PNG image with a side from 64 to 1024 pixels.`
   The portable Fearless ignores icons, so it does not run this check: a project with such an icon compiles there and fails only in the manager.
3. Choose the missing extensions (see **Autoselected extensions**).
The main.info would contain "" for a 'missing extension' and the main.info file is edited to contain the autoselected extension.
4. Materialise every icon in `.fearless_out/icons/`: the PNG copied out (it may be inside a zip, and the desktop needs a plain file), and on Windows the `.ico` made from it by `fileAssociations.Ico` in Commons. Two claims with the same icon type share the files.
Since the path in /icon is fully determined from the path of the autoloaded image; knowing one we can recover the other; keeping both would be redundant.

5. Rebuild the in memory map and reconcile (see **Reconciling**).
6. Create the missing shortcut files (see **Shortcut files**).

`projects.info` is unchanged: its `mains` field is the user's choice of which mains to run, not a copy of the compiled mains.

## The in memory map

The manager keeps `extension -> list of (project, main, icon)` in memory only. It builds it at start up from the `mains.info` of every registered `code` project, and rebuilds it after every compile, clean (the project's `.fearless_out` is gone, so it claims nothing), forget, and kind change away from `code`. A project whose `mains.info` is missing claims nothing.

## Autoselected extensions

A `Shortcut` without extension gets `fappNNN`, an `OpenWith` without extension gets `ffileNNN`, where `NNN` are three digits.

1. If the previous `mains.info` of the project gives this claim an autoselected extension, keep it: a shortcut file dragged to the desktop keeps working across compiles.
2. Else start from `Math.floorMod((alias+"::"+main+"::"+icon).hashCode(), 1000)`, with `alias` the project name in `projects.info` and `main` the qualified main name. `String.hashCode` is fixed by the Java specification, so the start is the same on every machine. The icon is part of the key so that `Shortcut[I1]` and `Shortcut[I2]` of one main start apart.
3. The number is free if no other claim in the in memory map uses that extension; else try the next one, wrapping from 999 to 000.
4. If all 1000 are taken:
   `No free extension is left for "base.Shortcut[hello.IconsBar]" of main "hello.Bar": all the 1000 extensions "fapp000" to "fapp999" are used by the projects of this manager. Give this Shortcut an explicit extension, for example "base.Shortcut[hello.IconsBar,\"bar\"]". 

Which project compiles first can decide who gets a number. This is accepted.

## Shortcut files

For each shortcut claim, an empty file `<name>.<extension>` in the top level of the project, next to the `.fearless` marker. `<name>` is the simple name of the main turned into a file name, the inverse of `AutoloadHandler.capFirst`: each uppercase letter becomes `_` and its lowercase, except the first letter of the name, which only becomes lowercase. `FooBar` becomes `foo_bar`, `_Foo` becomes `_foo`, `Foo_1` becomes `foo_1`, `FooBAR` becomes `foo_b_a_r`.

Since every file name in a project is lowercase, `fooBar.png` is not a valid asset name, so `foo_bar` is the only name that `capFirst` maps to `FooBar`. The inverse does not exist for every type name: type names can end in primes (`Foo'`), and can contain `_` followed by a lowercase letter (`Foo_bar`), which `capFirst` never produces. Such a main, one whose name turns into a Windows reserved name (`Con`), or two shortcut mains of the project giving the same file name and extension (`a.Bar` and `b.Bar`), fail the compile:
`Main "hello.Foo'" can not have a Shortcut: its name has no matching file name. A main with a Shortcut has a name that can be mapped to a file name like "FooBar" can be mapped to "foo_bar".

The shortcut file is a visible file of the project: it follows the project file rules, and the project checks see it. Creating it must not make the project read as modified: this is not a problem: the file is at top level and all the source and autoloaded assets must be inside some folder structure. (If today we do not exclude top level files to mark recompilation, we should) `Facts.cacheUpToDate` must skips the top level files. A shortcut file the user deleted is created again by the next compile; one the user moved out of the project stays where it is, and a new one appears.

## Reconciling

`FileAssociations.reconcile` (in Commons) takes the whole wanted set at once, so after each change of the in memory map the manager calls it with every extension: `.fearless` and every claimed extension, each as a `fileAssociations.Icon(extension with dot, ico, png)`. An extension claimed by one main uses its icon; an extension claimed by several mains uses the conflict icon. On Linux the `ico` field is not read; the manager passes the PNG there too. Step 5 of `reconcile` makes an unchanged set cost nothing, and it also runs at manager start up, as it does today for `.fearless`.

The checks of `reconcile` change nothing when they fail, so the previous associations stay in place. A failing `reconcile` after a compile makes that compile fail, and the in memory map goes back to the previous `mains.info` of that project (the manager keeps it until reconcile succeeded). To name the failing extension, the main claiming it and the program holding it, `reconcile` reports which extension failed which check instead of one text; the messages are those of `Violation.association*`, completed with the main.

The manager settles conflicts among the Fearless apps it manages; with the OS and every other program it needs a clear yes or no. An extension is either fully controlled by the manager or the compile fails loudly:
- An extension claimed by a program outside the Fearless family (`notOurs`): Follow `FileAssociations.reconcile`. So `OpenWith[I,"txt"]` fails on every machine where another program already opens `.txt` in a way that `FileAssociations.reconcile` would not snatch away. There is no mode that merely adds the manager to the programs offered for an extension.
- An extension with a Windows UserChoice (`userLocked`): 
Follow `FileAssociations.reconcile`, even when the UserChoice names this manager, exactly as for `.fearless`: an association the user pinned is one the manager could not take away later.
- An extension held by another Fearless identity (an older manager, the portable Fearless): Follow `FileAssociations.reconcile`, as for `.fearless`.

The portable Fearless reconciles `.fearless` under its own identity at every start, removing the manager's associations. That is accepted: at its next start the manager takes back every extension it manages.

## Opening a file

The desktop starts the manager with the file path as its only argument. Today such a message selects the file's parent folder, registering it as a project when it is not one: a shortcut file on the desktop would register the desktop. A message naming a regular file whose extension is in the in memory map is an open instead:

1. One main claims the extension: run that main, as the `run` message verb does (selecting the project, running only that main, `<needs compiling>` when the project is not compiled).
2. Several mains claim it: a small window lists them with their icons as `project::pkg.Main`, sorted, and runs the one picked; closing it runs nothing.
3. The main is already running: the manager shows the project and runs nothing more; a `Session` runs one job per project.
In the future, the manage will have to signal to the process to 'wake it up', for example its gui could come front, but we will design this API later.

A file with the `.fearless` extension, or with an extension nobody claims, keeps today's meaning.

## Delivering the file

`System.inputCursor` is designed as a linked list that grows when new input files arrive. Its `rt` implementation (`_InputCursor`) returns a fixed demo node today, and connecting it to real files is a separate unit of work. So in this unit the opened file does not reach the program: `OpenWith` and `Shortcut` behave the same on a double click, and differ only in the shortcut file. Feeding the file of a second double click to a main already running is also a later unit.

Receiving a file does not need its extension, but creating one does. A main with `OpenWith[I]` gets an extension such as `ffile042` chosen by the manager, and that choice varies between machines and compile orders; to save a document the user can later double click, the program must name it `<something>.ffile042`. So a later unit adds a way for a main to learn the extensions it claims, for example a `System` method.

## The manager window

For each project, per main:
- `Extensions this main opens`: icon and extension, for each claim that no other main claims.
- `Conflicting extensions`: icon, extension, and one other claimant with its icon and `project::pkg.Main`: the first in alphabetical order of `project::pkg.Main`, followed by `and N more` when there are more.
- The shortcut mains are shown by their icons; a double click on one runs that main.

## Conflict icon

The Fearless icon with a purple `?` on top of it, stored as a PNG in the standard library at `base/icons/conflict.png`, with its `.ico` next to it. The manager reads it from the stdLib folder of its application image.

It becomes the autoloaded type `base.IconsConflict`, and we need to support `base` with autoloaded assets. That needs work of its own: today `BaseLayer` compiles `base` with no autoloading and hands every file of the stdLib folder to the Frontend as source, and the asset paths are relative to a project root, not to the stdLib folder.

